# Keep build products out of the build context.
#
# Every Containerfile here does `COPY apexd/ /build/apexd/` and then builds it.
# A developer's local `cargo build` leaves ~440 MB of host-built artefacts in
# apexd/target, and without this they are shipped into the context and land on
# top of the container's own target directory — host rustc and glibc output
# seeding a container build. CI never noticed because a fresh checkout has no
# target/; a local build is where it bites, and "works in CI, wrong locally" is
# the harder direction to debug.
#
# ── ANCHORED. `**/target/` is FORBIDDEN here, and this is the second time ───
#
# This file shipped `**/target/` and it broke every image build. The pattern
# also matches `apexd/apex-backup-core/src/target/`, which is a SOURCE module —
# the backup destinations, fs/ssh/bucket — so `COPY apexd/` delivered a tree
# whose lib.rs says `pub mod target;` with no such file, and cargo answered:
#
#     error[E0583]: file not found for module `target`
#     error: could not compile `apex-backup-core` (lib)
#
# The `base` job then exited 101 having produced no image. Run 35608265004.
#
# .gitignore learned this first and its comment records it: a bare `target/`
# hid that same directory from git for the life of the repository, and the fix
# was to anchor each real cargo output directory by path. This file now does
# the same, and tests/check-containerignore-anchored.sh refuses any unanchored
# `target` pattern so the lesson does not have to be learned a third time.
#
# Only apexd/ and windows-installer/ carry a Cargo.toml with its own build
# directory; every other Cargo.toml under apexd/ is a workspace member and
# shares apexd/target/.
apexd/target/
apexd/target-clippy/
windows-installer/target/

.git/
